dium", "notes": ["Incumbent Mike Flood (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's 1st District pairs Republican incumbent Mike Flood, seeking a third full term, with Democrat Chris Backemeyer, a first-time congressional candidate. The seat leans Republican by about six points, and most expert raters place it in Likely R territory, though a few call it Lean R. Flood holds a clear fundraising edge with $2.8 million raised, though Backemeyer's $1.2 million is strong for a challenger here.\n\nThe race has tightened in recent polling: two Democratic-sponsored surveys from September and October show the candidates essentially tied, a sharp shift from spring polls that had Flood ahead by seven or more. Prediction markets still favor Flood at roughly 78 percent. The contest has drawn active local coverage, including debates in September and October, suggesting a genuinely competitive race despite the district's lean.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1466, "margin": -6.26}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1237, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1206, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2177, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1689, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1196, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1777, "margin": -6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2315, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.1394, "margin": -7.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.1156, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.114, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.1142, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.1121, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.173, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-06", "p_dem": 0.1374, "margin": -6.6}, {"date": "2026-10-07", "p_dem": 0.1133, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-10-08", "p_dem": 0.1024, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-09", "p_dem": 0.1161, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-10-10", "p_dem": 0.106